            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="true" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <HouseholdOperatingConditions numberOfOccupants="5" coolingSeasonMonths="0" isClothesWasherEnergySaver="true" isLightingEnergySaver="true" areAppliancesEnergySaver="false">
                <Thermostat heatingDaytime="20" heatingNighttime="20" cooling="25" />
            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="1" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="1" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage. There are some areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets and light switches on your exterior walls, weather stripping around windows &amp; doors, around light fixtures, and along the baseboards (between the floor and wall). 



I recommend you install an 'outlet and switch' gasket on all electrical outlets that are located on an exterior wall.  Caulking can also be used to seal up around the light fixtures to stop air leakage. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 280&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Your heating system requires air to be mixed with fuel to burn and heat your house. Without combustion air, your heating system is using the house air for combustion which may not be adequate for an efficient burn. The combustion air duct installed in your furnace room is open and freely drops cold air into the room all year long whether its needed or not. 



I recommend you install an electric damper on the combustion air duct and tie it to the operation of your heating system and water heater. This will ensure fresh air will only be introduced into the room when the heating system or water heater is operating. 







&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 



By installing an electric damper on the combustion air duct, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 40&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are no rebates for installing a damper on the combustion air duct, however it will increase your homes airtightness and may qualify you for an Air Sealing rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. As a future upgrade consideration, when you are replacing the siding of the house, I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 920&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;24.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;















&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older and do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all 9 windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 550&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;14.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

















&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

As your home currently has excess air leakage, fresh air has been introduced into your home naturally. As you begin sealing up the house (and making it more air tight) by eliminating air leakage, the naturally introduced fresh air will begin to be reduced. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air.



I recommend you install an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom exhaust fan connected to a humidistat in your bathroom upstairs. This will help control any excess moisture within the home, and these models of bathroom exhaust fans will incorporate a built-in damper to reduce any air leakage when it is not operating.





&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

When you are considering replacing your hot water heater, I recommend that you replace your Natural Gas hot water heater tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 150&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;















&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current natural gas fired furnace was originally rated at 81.7% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing furnace.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 850&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;23.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;









&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



As your home currently has excess air leakage, fresh air has been introduced into your home naturally. As you begin sealing up the house (and making it more air tight) by eliminating air leakage, the naturally introduced fresh air will begin to be reduced. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air.



I recommend you install an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom exhaust fan connected to a humidistat in your bathroom upstairs. This will help control any excess moisture within the home, and these models of bathroom exhaust fans will incorporate a built-in damper to reduce any air leakage when it is not operating.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Inuvik&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:

Electricity - $18.9 min, $0.313 per kWh

Natural Gas - $37.21 per GJ

Oil  - $1.21 per litre 

Propane - $1.52 per litre

Wood - $472 per cord 

Pellets - $11.03 per bag



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist&lt;/b&gt;

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



&lt;font color="lightseagreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
